            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Emancipation</strong><br>\nBooker T. Washington was born into slavery in 1856 in Virginia. With the end of the Civil War in 1865, Washington was freed along with millions of other African Americans. He then worked in salt furnaces and coal mines in West Virginia to earn money for his education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education at Hampton Institute</strong><br>\nDetermined to get an education, Washington attended Hampton Normal and Agricultural Institute in Virginia, now known as Hampton University. He excelled as a student, and his hard work and determination led to a recommendation to lead the new Tuskegee Institute in Alabama.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of Tuskegee Institute</strong><br>\nWashington founded the Tuskegee Normal and Industrial Institute in 1881 and served as its first leader. Under his direction, the school focused on providing African Americans with a practical education that emphasized industrial training.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Atlanta Compromise Speech</strong><br>\nIn 1895, Washington delivered his famous \"Atlanta Compromise\" speech, which outlined his vision for the progress of African Americans through education and entrepreneurship, rather than through confrontation over segregation and disenfranchisement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy on Race Relations</strong><br>\nWashington advocated for a philosophy of self-help, industrial or vocational training, and economic self-reliance for African Americans. He believed that it was through education and economic progress that African Americans would gain respect and equality in American society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Opposition</strong><br>\nWashington faced criticism from other African American leaders, like W.E.B. Du Bois, who disagreed with his accommodating stance on civil rights. They argued for immediate equality and criticized Washington for being too conciliatory.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
