          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Achievements and Honors</strong><br>\nDuring his lifetime, Washington became the most prominent African American leader. He advised Presidents and was the first African American invited to the White House. His autobiography, \"Up from Slavery,\" became a bestseller and is still widely read today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Outreach and Influence</strong><br>\nWashington was instrumental in the establishment of the National Negro Business League and became an influential figure in both black and white communities across the United States. His network of supporters helped him fund Tuskegee and other educational projects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nBooker T. Washington died on November 14, 1915, leaving behind a legacy of substantial contributions to African American education and leadership. He is remembered for his pragmatic approach to racial uplift and his unyielding commitment to the education of African Americans in the post-Reconstruction South.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
